ance for homeowners and renters. The company also writes auto-related liability and physical damage insurance for comprising dump trucks, log trucks, garbage trucks, tractors, trailers, straight trucks, tow trucks and wreckers, vans, pick-up trucks, and autos; business-related general liability and commercial property insurance for small businesses; and workers' compensation insurance for the transportation industry. In addition, it offers other specialty property-casualty insurance and provides related services; personal property reinsurance products; and involved in investment activities. It sells its products through independent insurance agencies, as well as online and over the phone. The Progressive Corporation was founded in 1937 and is headquartered in Mayfield, Ohio.","ceo_bio":"Ms. Susan Patricia Griffith | President, CEO & Director | Age 61 | Compensation: $6.7M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 70K people. Revenue per employee of $1.3M ranks in the top quartile among Financial Services peers, with revenue growing 12%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $246K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $2K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 5\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (3/10).","financial_summary":["Progressive Corporation (The) is a large-cap financial services business with a $136.6B market cap on $87.7B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +21% over the past three years and +15% annually across the full decade, the kind of sustained demand persistence that marks a compounder.","Returns on capital run at 23% against a 7% cost of capital \u2014 a 15-point spread that is the clearest signature of an economic moat.","The business throws off $17.2B in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 20 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.55\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably conservative at 0.0\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and interest coverage of 52\u00d7 is fortress-like.","Shareholder yield of 2.1% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Operating margins 13% below sector median \u2014 a cost disadvantage the moat cannot hide","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Trading near 52-week highs \u2014 the market has already recognised the story","sev":"amber","cat":"valuation","magnitude":0.32587160684420363},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Piotroski score 4/9 \u2014 below the median for a healthy business","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Altman Z in the grey zone \u2014 financial health warrants monitoring","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Insiders are net sellers (100% sell ratio, net $21.7M) \u2014 the people closest to the business are reducing their personal stake","sev":"amber","cat":"insider"},{"text":"Credit cycles and regulatory capital \u2014 losses accumulate invisibly in good years and appear all at once, and the time to worry is when underwriting standards are loosest.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Interest rate regime shifts \u2014 a rapid move in rates can invert the yield curve, crush bond portfolios, and trigger deposit flight simultaneously.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Trading at a 38% margin of safety to estimated fair value \u2014 a real cushion against errors in the estimate","Market is pricing in lower growth than the company has historically delivered","16 consecutive years of dividend payments \u2014 an operating record that makes the dividend claim credible","Growing faster than most Financial Services peers \u2014 taking share inside the profit pool"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"Why Is Progressive (PGR) Up 10.2% Since Last Earnings Report?","source":"zacks.com","link":"https://www.zacks.com/stock/news/3029684/why-is-progressive-pgr-up-10-2-since-last-earnings-report?cid=CS-ZC-FT-realtime_blog-3029684","date":"2026-11-13","timestamp":1794583803.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.0,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":["earnings"]},{"title":"Progressive (PGR) Stock Could Be Stretched After A 190% Five Year Run","source":"simplywall.st","link":"https://simplywall.st/stocks/us/insurance/nyse-pgr/progressive/news/progressive-pgr-stock-could-be-stretched-after-a-190-five-ye","date":"2026-11-13","timestamp":1794540685.97,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.813,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Progressive (PGR) Finishes Snapshot Pro Rollout For Commercial Auto Customers","source":"finance.yahoo.com","link":"https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/progressive-pgr-finishes-snapshot-pro-221046509.html","date":"2026-11-12","timestamp":1794521446.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.485,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":["product"]},{"title":"Is Progressive (PGR) Fully Valued On Snapshot Pro Rollout Momentum?","source":"simplywall.st","link":"https://simplywall.st/stocks/us/insurance/nyse-pgr/progressive/news/is-progressive-pgr-fully-valued-on-snapshot-pro-rollout-mome","date":"2026-11-12","timestamp":1794446990.042,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.968,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":["product"]},{"title":"Why Progressive (PGR) is a Top Value Stock for the Long-Term","source":"zacks.com","link":"https://www.zacks.com/stock/news/3028134/why-progressive-pgr-is-a-top-val